202	<!-- ═══════════════════════ pybind11 ═══════════════════════ -->
203	<div class="term-card" id="pybind11">
204	  <div class="term-header">
205	    <span class="term-name">pybind11</span>
206	    <span class="term-type type-tool">Library</span>
207	  </div>
208	
209	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
210	  <p class="definition">A header-only C++11 library that exposes C++ types and functions to Python (and vice versa) using template metaprogramming. There is no separate code-generation step — the entire binding infrastructure is resolved at compile time from template instantiations. The library lives in <code>.hpp</code> files; you include them and link against Python's C API.</p>
211	
212	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
213	  <pre><span class="cmt">// cpp/oil_volume/bindings.cpp</span>
214	<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;pybind11/pybind11.h&gt;</span>
215	<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;pybind11/stl.h&gt;</span>   <span class="cmt">// enables std::vector ↔ list conversion</span>
216	
217	namespace py = pybind11;   <span class="cmt">// conventional alias</span></pre>
218	
219	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
220	  <p class="usage-sentence">"We use pybind11 to expose the C++ rolling_mean kernel as a Python callable with no hand-written PyArg_ParseTuple boilerplate."</p>
221	
222	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
223	  <div class="contrast-box">
224	    <strong>vs. CPython C API directly:</strong> the raw C API requires manual reference counting (<code>Py_INCREF/DECREF</code>), type checking, and argument parsing — hundreds of lines for a simple function. pybind11 reduces this to one <code>m.def()</code> call.<br><br>
225	    <strong>vs. ctypes:</strong> ctypes calls C functions at runtime via dynamic linking. pybind11 is compiled into the extension and supports C++ features (overloading, exceptions, STL containers, classes) that ctypes cannot handle.<br><br>
226	    <strong>vs. Cython:</strong> Cython generates C code from a Python-like DSL. pybind11 is pure C++17, which is preferable when the code you are wrapping is already written in C++.
227	  </div>
228	</div>
229	
230	<!-- ═══════════════════════ PYBIND11_MODULE ═══════════════════════ -->
231	<div class="term-card" id="pybind11-module">
232	  <div class="term-header">
233	    <span class="term-name">PYBIND11_MODULE</span>
234	    <span class="term-type type-macro">C++ Macro</span>
235	  </div>
236	
237	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
238	  <p class="definition">A C preprocessor macro that generates the <code>extern "C" PyObject* PyInit_&lt;name&gt;()</code> entry-point function that Python's import machinery calls after <code>dlopen()</code>-ing the shared library. The macro takes two arguments: the module name (must match the compiled filename and the Python import name) and a variable name bound to the <code>py::module_</code> object used to register functions, classes, and constants.</p>
239	
240	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
241	  <pre><span class="cmt">// cpp/oil_volume/bindings.cpp</span>
242	<span class="fn">PYBIND11_MODULE</span>(oil_volume_cpp, m) {
243	    m.doc() = <span class="str">"C++ core for oil-volume metrics (pybind11 bridge)"</span>;
244	    m.<span class="fn">def</span>(<span class="str">"rolling_mean"</span>, &amp;rolling_mean,
245	         py::<span class="fn">arg</span>(<span class="str">"values"</span>), py::<span class="fn">arg</span>(<span class="str">"window"</span>),
246	         <span class="str">"docstring..."</span>);
247	}</pre>
248	
249	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
250	  <p class="usage-sentence">"The PYBIND11_MODULE block is the only piece of the file that knows about Python — the rolling_mean C++ function above it is pure, testable C++."</p>
251	
252	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
253	  <div class="contrast-box">
254	    <strong>vs. PyMODINIT_FUNC:</strong> the raw C API equivalent. You must manually populate a <code>PyMethodDef[]</code> array and call <code>PyModule_Create</code>. PYBIND11_MODULE does this for you, with type safety.<br><br>
255	    <strong>Name coupling:</strong> the string <code>oil_volume_cpp</code> passed to the macro must exactly match (1) the CMake target name in <code>pybind11_add_module</code>, (2) the filename stem of the built <code>.so</code>, and (3) the Python import name. A mismatch gives <code>ImportError: dynamic module does not define module export function</code>.
256	  </div>
257	</div>
258	
259	<!-- ═══════════════════════ pybind11_add_module ═══════════════════════ -->
260	<div class="term-card" id="pybind11-add-module">
261	  <div class="term-header">
262	    <span class="term-name">pybind11_add_module</span>
263	    <span class="term-type type-cmake">CMake Function</span>
264	  </div>
265	
266	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
267	  <p class="definition">A CMake function provided by pybind11's CMake integration that wraps <code>add_library(name SHARED ...)</code> with all Python-extension-specific settings: the correct ABI-tagged suffix, no <code>lib</code> prefix, Python header linkage, <code>-fvisibility=hidden</code> to prevent symbol collisions, and LTO in Release mode.</p>
268	
269	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
270	  <pre><span class="cmt"># cpp/oil_volume/CMakeLists.txt</span>
271	<span class="fn">pybind11_add_module</span>(oil_volume_cpp bindings.cpp)
272	<span class="fn">target_include_directories</span>(oil_volume_cpp PRIVATE ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR})</pre>
273	
274	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
275	  <p class="usage-sentence">"Without pybind11_add_module you would have to manually set SUFFIX, PREFIX, and link flags for every platform; the function encapsulates all of that."</p>
276	
277	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
278	  <div class="contrast-box">
279	    <strong>vs. add_library(SHARED):</strong> plain <code>add_library</code> produces a <code>liboil_volume_cpp.so</code> with no Python-specific flags. Python will not find it (<code>ImportError</code>) because the name and suffix are wrong.<br><br>
280	    <strong>vs. Python's distutils Extension:</strong> the old <code>setup.py</code> approach. <code>pybind11_add_module</code> is its CMake equivalent, but with full IDE, cross-compilation, and generator support.
281	  </div>
282	</div>
283	
284	<!-- ═══════════════════════ FetchContent ═══════════════════════ -->
285	<div class="term-card" id="fetchcontent">
286	  <div class="term-header">
287	    <span class="term-name">FetchContent</span>
288	    <span class="term-type type-cmake">CMake Module</span>
289	  </div>
290	
291	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
292	  <p class="definition">A CMake built-in module (available since CMake 3.11) that downloads external dependencies at configure time — before compilation — and makes their CMake targets available as if they were a local <code>add_subdirectory</code>. <code>FetchContent_Declare</code> registers a dependency; <code>FetchContent_MakeAvailable</code> downloads and configures it. Fetched sources go into the build tree under <code>_deps/</code>.</p>
293	
294	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
295	  <pre><span class="cmt"># CMakeLists.txt — fallback if pip-installed pybind11 not found</span>
296	<span class="fn">FetchContent_Declare</span>(
297	    pybind11
298	    GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/pybind/pybind11.git
299	    GIT_TAG        v2.13.6          <span class="cmt"># pin exact version</span>
300	)
301	<span class="fn">FetchContent_MakeAvailable</span>(pybind11)
302	<span class="cmt"># After this line, pybind11_add_module is available</span></pre>
303	
304	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
305	  <p class="usage-sentence">"FetchContent pins pybind11 to v2.13.6 so a fresh clone in CI always builds against a known-good version without needing a system package manager."</p>
306	
307	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
308	  <div class="contrast-box">
309	    <strong>vs. find_package:</strong> <code>find_package</code> looks for an already-installed package (e.g. from pip or the system). <code>FetchContent</code> downloads the package if it is not found. This repo uses both: try <code>find_package</code> first (fast, offline), fall back to <code>FetchContent</code>.<br><br>
310	    <strong>vs. ExternalProject_Add:</strong> <code>ExternalProject_Add</code> builds the dependency at build time (not configure time), so its targets are not available in the same CMake scope. <code>FetchContent</code> is simpler for header-only or CMake-native dependencies like pybind11.
311	  </div>
312	</div>
313	
314	<!-- ═══════════════════════ scikit-build-core ═══════════════════════ -->
315	<div class="term-card" id="scikit-build-core">
316	  <div class="term-header">
317	    <span class="term-name">scikit-build-core</span>
318	    <span class="term-type type-tool">Build Backend</span>
319	  </div>
320	
321	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
322	  <p class="definition">A PEP 517 build backend for Python packages that contain CMake-built native extensions. When pip calls it to build a wheel, it invokes CMake with the correct Python interpreter, builds the native targets, and packages the resulting <code>.so</code> files alongside Python source into a wheel. Declared as <code>build-backend = "scikit_build_core.build"</code> in <code>pyproject.toml</code>.</p>
323	
324	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
325	  <pre><span class="cmt"># pyproject.toml</span>
326	[build-system]
327	requires = [<span class="str">"scikit-build-core>=0.9"</span>, <span class="str">"pybind11>=2.12"</span>]
328	build-backend = <span class="str">"scikit_build_core.build"</span>
329	
330	[tool.scikit-build.cmake]
331	build-type = <span class="str">"Release"</span>
332	args = [<span class="str">"-DPYBIND11_FINDPYTHON=ON"</span>]</pre>
333	
334	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
335	  <p class="usage-sentence">"scikit-build-core means a single pip install . builds both the C++ extension and installs the Python package — no separate cmake and make step needed."</p>
336	
337	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
338	  <div class="contrast-box">
339	    <strong>vs. setuptools + setup.py:</strong> the old approach used <code>setuptools.Extension</code> to compile C extensions, but had poor CMake support and required a <code>setup.py</code> file.<br><br>
340	    <strong>vs. meson-python:</strong> an alternative backend that uses the Meson build system instead of CMake. Both are PEP 517 compliant; choose based on your build system preference.<br><br>
341	    <strong>vs. scikit-build (original):</strong> the predecessor to scikit-build-core. scikit-build-core is the modern rewrite with better PEP 517 compliance and no <code>setup.py</code> requirement.
342	  </div>
343	</div>
344	
345	<!-- ═══════════════════════ PYBIND11_FINDPYTHON ═══════════════════════ -->
346	<div class="term-card" id="pybind11-findpython">
347	  <div class="term-header">
348	    <span class="term-name">PYBIND11_FINDPYTHON</span>
349	    <span class="term-type type-cmake">CMake Variable</span>
350	  </div>
351	
352	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
353	  <p class="definition">A CMake cache variable that controls which Python-discovery module pybind11 uses. When set to <code>ON</code> (before <code>find_package(pybind11)</code> is called), pybind11 uses CMake's modern <code>FindPython3</code> module rather than the deprecated <code>FindPythonLibs</code>. <code>FindPython3</code> derives header and library paths from any Python executable you point it at (via <code>-DPython3_EXECUTABLE=...</code>), making it work correctly in virtual environments and uv-managed Pythons.</p>
354	
355	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
356	  <pre><span class="cmt"># CMakeLists.txt — must appear before find_package(pybind11)</span>
357	<span class="fn">set</span>(PYBIND11_FINDPYTHON ON)
358	
359	<span class="cmt"># pyproject.toml — passed through scikit-build-core to cmake</span>
360	[tool.scikit-build.cmake]
361	args = [<span class="str">"-DPYBIND11_FINDPYTHON=ON"</span>]</pre>
362	
363	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
364	  <p class="usage-sentence">"Without PYBIND11_FINDPYTHON=ON, CMake would pick up the system Python headers at /usr/include/python3.x instead of the venv's bundled headers, producing a .so that fails to import with an ABI mismatch."</p>
365	
366	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
367	  <div class="contrast-box">
368	    <strong>PYBIND11_FINDPYTHON=ON</strong> → uses <code>FindPython3</code>, respects <code>-DPython3_EXECUTABLE</code>, works with venvs and uv.<br>
369	    <strong>PYBIND11_FINDPYTHON=OFF (default)</strong> → uses <code>FindPythonLibs</code>, searches system paths, may find the wrong Python or fail entirely in non-standard environments.
370	  </div>
371	</div>
372	
373	<!-- ═══════════════════════ editable install ═══════════════════════ -->
374	<div class="term-card" id="editable-install">
375	  <div class="term-header">
376	    <span class="term-name">editable install</span>
377	    <span class="term-type type-py">Python Packaging</span>
378	  </div>
379	
380	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
381	  <p class="definition">A pip install mode (<code>pip install -e .</code>) where Python source changes in the repo are immediately importable without reinstalling. pip places a <code>.pth</code> file or <code>__editable__</code> hook in <code>site-packages</code> that adds the source directory to <code>sys.path</code>. For packages with native extensions, the extension is compiled once at install time and must be manually rebuilt on C++ changes — only Python files are "live".</p>
382	
383	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
384	  <pre><span class="cmt"># Development workflow</span>
385	pip install -e .
386	<span class="cmt"># Python changes: live immediately
387	# C++ changes: must re-run pip install -e . or ./scripts/build_cpp.sh</span>
388	
389	<span class="cmt"># After build, the .so lives in src/oil_alpha/ and is importable via PYTHONPATH=src</span></pre>
390	
391	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
392	  <p class="usage-sentence">"We use pip install -e . so we can edit oil_alpha/*.py and test the changes instantly, while the compiled oil_volume_cpp.so remains in-place until the C++ is changed."</p>
393	
394	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
395	  <div class="contrast-box">
396	    <strong>vs. regular install (pip install .):</strong> copies source into <code>site-packages</code>; Python changes are not reflected until you reinstall.<br><br>
397	    <strong>vs. PYTHONPATH hack:</strong> manually setting <code>PYTHONPATH=src</code> achieves the same for pure Python but does not handle the native extension placement. Editable install handles both.
398	  </div>
399	</div>
400	
401	<!-- ═══════════════════════ ABI ═══════════════════════ -->
402	<div class="term-card" id="abi">
403	  <div class="term-header">
404	    <span class="term-name">ABI</span>
405	    <span class="term-type type-concept">Concept</span>
406	  </div>
407	
408	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
409	  <p class="definition">Application Binary Interface — the compiled-code-level contract between a program and its dependencies: calling conventions, data layout, name mangling, and in Python's case, the layout of CPython internal structs (like <code>PyObject</code>). A Python C extension compiled for CPython 3.12 cannot be loaded by CPython 3.11 because the struct layouts differ. The ABI version is encoded in the extension's filename suffix (<code>.cpython-312-x86_64-linux-gnu.so</code>) so Python can reject incompatible files before attempting to load them.</p>
410	
411	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
412	  <pre><span class="cmt"># Actual built file name:</span>
413	oil_volume_cpp.cpython-312-x86_64-linux-gnu.so
414	<span class="cmt">#               ──┬──  ───┬──── ────────┬────
415	#                 │       │             └─ OS / platform
416	#                 │       └─ architecture
417	#                 └─ Python implementation + version (ABI)</span></pre>
418	
419	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
420	  <p class="usage-sentence">"After upgrading from Python 3.12 to 3.13, the existing .so has the wrong ABI tag and gives ImportError — we must rerun pip install -e . to recompile."</p>
421	
422	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
423	  <div class="contrast-box">
424	    <strong>vs. Python Limited API (Py_LIMITED_API):</strong> compiling with <code>Py_LIMITED_API</code> produces a <code>.abi3.so</code> that is compatible with all Python 3.x versions &ge; the minimum you specify. This trades some performance (can't use version-specific struct fields) for portability. This repo does not use the Limited API.
425	  </div>
426	</div>
427	
428	<!-- ═══════════════════════ shared object ═══════════════════════ -->
429	<div class="term-card" id="shared-object">
430	  <div class="term-header">
431	    <span class="term-name">shared object (.so)</span>
432	    <span class="term-type type-concept">OS Concept</span>
433	  </div>
434	
435	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
436	  <p class="definition">On Linux (and macOS as <code>.dylib</code>), a shared object is a compiled native library loaded at runtime by the dynamic linker (<code>ld.so</code>). A Python C extension is a shared object with a <code>PyInit_</code> entry point that Python calls via <code>dlopen()</code>. The OS maps the library's code into the process's virtual address space and resolves external symbol references.</p>
437	
438	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
439	  <pre><span class="cmt"># CMake produces this file and places it in src/oil_alpha/:</span>
440	oil_volume_cpp.cpython-312-x86_64-linux-gnu.so
441	
442	<span class="cmt"># Verify it exports the correct symbol:</span>
443	nm -D oil_volume_cpp.cpython-312-x86_64-linux-gnu.so | grep PyInit</pre>
444	
445	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
446	  <p class="usage-sentence">"The build script sets -DCMAKE_LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY=src/oil_alpha so the .so lands where Python can find it without extra sys.path manipulation."</p>
447	
448	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
449	  <div class="contrast-box">
450	    <strong>vs. static library (.a):</strong> a static library is linked into the executable at compile time; all symbols are baked in. A shared object is loaded at runtime and can be updated independently of the Python interpreter.<br><br>
451	    <strong>vs. .pyd (Windows):</strong> on Windows, Python extensions use the <code>.pyd</code> extension but are structurally identical DLLs. pybind11 and CMake handle this platform difference automatically.
452	  </div>
453	</div>
454	
455	<!-- ═══════════════════════ parity test ═══════════════════════ -->
456	<div class="term-card" id="parity-test">
457	  <div class="term-header">
458	    <span class="term-name">parity test</span>
459	    <span class="term-type type-concept">Testing Pattern</span>
460	  </div>
461	
462	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
463	  <p class="definition">A test that verifies a C++ (or other optimised) implementation produces the same output as a trusted reference implementation (typically Python/NumPy) to within a specified floating-point tolerance. It serves as a correctness gate (catches logic bugs in C++) and a regression guard (catches subtle numerical changes from compiler upgrades or algorithm modifications). The tolerance is chosen to be tight enough to catch errors but loose enough to tolerate valid floating-point rounding differences between implementations.</p>
464	
465	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
466	  <pre><span class="cmt"># tests/test_cpp_bridge.py</span>
467	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_rolling_mean_parity</span>(cpp_module):
468	    values = [<span class="num">1.0</span>, <span class="num">2.0</span>, <span class="num">3.0</span>, ..., <span class="num">10.0</span>]
469	    cpp_result = cpp_module.rolling_mean(values, <span class="num">3</span>)
470	    np_result  = _numpy_rolling_mean(values, <span class="num">3</span>)
471	    <span class="kw">for</span> i, (c, n) <span class="kw">in</span> enumerate(zip(cpp_result, np_result)):
472	        <span class="kw">assert</span> abs(c - n) &lt; <span class="num">1e-9</span></pre>
473	
474	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
475	  <p class="usage-sentence">"The parity test at 1e-9 guarantees that the rolling_mean C++ kernel produces the same trading signal as the NumPy reference — essential before trusting C++ numbers in live strategies."</p>
476	
477	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
478	  <div class="contrast-box">
479	    <strong>vs. unit test:</strong> a unit test checks correctness in isolation with known inputs/outputs. A parity test cross-validates two implementations against each other — it can catch bugs that both implementations share with third-party data if combined with known-correct reference values.<br><br>
480	    <strong>vs. benchmark test:</strong> a benchmark measures speed, not correctness. Parity tests measure correctness. In HFT you need both: parity first, then benchmark to verify the C++ is actually faster.
481	  </div>
482	</div>
483	
484	<!-- ═══════════════════════ type caster ═══════════════════════ -->
485	<div class="term-card" id="type-caster">
486	  <div class="term-header">
487	    <span class="term-name">type caster</span>
488	    <span class="term-type type-concept">pybind11 Concept</span>
489	  </div>
490	
491	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
492	  <p class="definition">A template specialisation in pybind11 that converts a C++ type to a Python object and back. Type casters are resolved entirely at compile time — there is no runtime reflection. Built-in casters handle primitives (<code>int</code>, <code>double</code>, <code>bool</code>). Including <code>&lt;pybind11/stl.h&gt;</code> adds casters for standard containers. Including <code>&lt;pybind11/numpy.h&gt;</code> adds casters for NumPy arrays. You can write custom casters for your own types.</p>
493	
494	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
495	  <pre><span class="cmt">// Activated by #include &lt;pybind11/stl.h&gt;</span>
496	<span class="cmt">// Python list[float] → std::vector&lt;double&gt; (before C++ call)</span>
497	<span class="cmt">// std::vector&lt;double&gt; → Python list[float] (after C++ returns)</span>
498	
499	m.<span class="fn">def</span>(<span class="str">"rolling_mean"</span>,
500	    &amp;rolling_mean,            <span class="cmt">// takes std::vector&lt;double&gt;, returns std::vector&lt;double&gt;</span>
501	    py::<span class="fn">arg</span>(<span class="str">"values"</span>),       <span class="cmt">// caller passes a Python list</span>
502	    py::<span class="fn">arg</span>(<span class="str">"window"</span>)        <span class="cmt">// caller passes a Python int</span>
503	);</pre>
504	
505	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
506	  <p class="usage-sentence">"The type caster from pybind11/stl.h transparently converts the Python list the caller passes into a std::vector<double> before the C++ function even runs."</p>
507	
508	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
509	  <div class="contrast-box">
510	    <strong>vs. manual conversion:</strong> without type casters you would call <code>PyList_GetItem</code>, <code>PyFloat_AsDouble</code>, etc. manually for each argument — error-prone and verbose.<br><br>
511	    <strong>vs. NumPy buffer protocol:</strong> for large arrays, copying to <code>std::vector</code> is wasteful. Using <code>&lt;pybind11/numpy.h&gt;</code> gives you a zero-copy <code>py::array_t&lt;double&gt;</code> type caster — the C++ code reads directly from the NumPy buffer without any data copy.
512	  </div>
